[38;5;231m⏺[39m I found it — [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m, consumed by [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m. One-character-class change, but it's a
  security-posture value, so I want your explicit go-ahead before editing.

  The consequence: 30m → 8h (480) is a 16× widening of the window in which a stolen session token, an unattended
  logged-in browser, or a hijacked session stays valid. Revocation-on-expiry effectively stops protecting you for a full
  working day. If the goal is "users shouldn't get logged out mid-task," idle timeout + sliding renewal usually gets
  that without extending the absolute lifetime — but that's a design change, not this one-liner.

